WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Two acids, two pH readings.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.080
An unknown acid, H X, at zero point

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.120
zero five zero zero moles per litre, reads

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.640
two point seven eight.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.840
Ethanoic acid at zero point five zero zero

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.040
moles per litre reads two point five two,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.320
so it's more acidic.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.440
Does that make ethanoic acid the stronger acid?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:22.960
Not necessarily.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.320
Its solution is ten times more concentrated.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.280
To compare strength fairly, you need a number

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.520
that doesn't depend on concentration.

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:35.213
You've met that number in Module 5: K

00:00:35.374 --> 00:00:39.133
a, the equilibrium constant for the acid ionising,

00:00:39.214 --> 00:00:42.733
hydronium times the anion, over the whole acid.

00:00:43.054 --> 00:00:45.853
K a doesn't change with concentration, and the

00:00:45.894 --> 00:00:48.653
bigger it is, the further the acid ionises

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.653
at the same concentration.

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:53.293
For ethanoic acid, K a is one point

00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:55.693
eight times ten to the minus five.

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:57.533
But we don't know K a for H

00:00:57.694 --> 00:00:58.493
X.

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:00.413
We can find it from the pH, by

00:01:00.494 --> 00:01:02.253
running the ICE table backwards.

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:06.746
Here's the task: find K a for H

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:07.626
X.

00:01:07.947 --> 00:01:09.306
First, the hydronium.

00:01:09.787 --> 00:01:12.906
pH is the negative log of hydronium, so

00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:15.546
hydronium is ten to the minus two point

00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:18.426
seven eight: one point six six times ten

00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:20.826
to the minus three moles per litre.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.266
Keep those digits for now.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.626
That's only about three percent of the concentration,

00:01:25.747 --> 00:01:27.946
so H X really is weak.

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:30.586
A strong acid would give hydronium equal to

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:31.706
its concentration.

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:35.333
Now the table, but this time we know

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:36.693
the bottom row first.

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:40.693
Each H X that ionises makes one hydronium

00:01:40.854 --> 00:01:44.133
and one X minus, so X minus is

00:01:44.214 --> 00:01:46.613
also one point six six times ten to

00:01:46.654 --> 00:01:47.493
the minus three.

00:01:48.214 --> 00:01:50.933
The acid left is zero point zero five

00:01:51.094 --> 00:01:54.293
zero zero minus that, zero point zero four

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:55.253
eight three.

00:01:55.574 --> 00:01:58.213
So K a is one point six six

00:01:58.374 --> 00:02:01.333
times ten to the minus three, squared, over

00:02:01.414 --> 00:02:04.613
zero point zero four eight three: five point

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:06.693
seven times ten to the minus five.

00:02:07.254 --> 00:02:10.053
That's two significant figures, because the pH had

00:02:10.134 --> 00:02:11.253
two decimal places.

00:02:11.894 --> 00:02:14.773
We measured the hydronium, so nothing is approximated

00:02:14.854 --> 00:02:15.253
here.

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:18.946
So H X has a K a about

00:02:19.107 --> 00:02:21.506
three times bigger than ethanoic acid's.

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:24.386
H X is the stronger acid, even though

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:25.586
its pH is higher.

00:02:25.987 --> 00:02:27.826
Its solution is just more dilute.

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:30.466
pH describes one solution.

00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:33.186
K a describes the acid itself.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.280
K a values are awkward, full of negative

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.320
powers of ten.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.640
So, just as pH tidies up hydronium, chemists

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.960
use p K a, the negative log of

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:45.840
K a.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.240
For H X, p K a is four

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.200
point two four, and for ethanoic acid, four

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.240
point seven four.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.080
Decimal places follow the same rule as pH:

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.800
two significant figures in K a, so two

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.800
decimal places in p K a.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.320
The minus sign flips the direction.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.880
A bigger K a gives a smaller p

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.440
K a, so the stronger acid has the

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.560
lower p K a.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.880
And each step of one in p K

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.040
a is a factor of ten in K

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:15.360
a.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.240
H X and ethanoic acid are half a

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.440
unit apart, and ten to the power of

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.120
a half is about three, not five.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.840
To go back, K a is ten to

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.440
the minus p K a.

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:31.386
This is how K a separates strong acids

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:32.626
from weak ones.

00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:35.226
A weak acid has molecules and ions in

00:03:35.307 --> 00:03:38.106
equilibrium, so its K a can be measured,

00:03:38.147 --> 00:03:40.266
and it's much less than one, which gives

00:03:40.347 --> 00:03:42.146
a positive p K a.

00:03:42.587 --> 00:03:44.746
As you saw in Module 5, a strong

00:03:44.827 --> 00:03:48.186
acid ionises completely, so there are essentially no

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:50.106
molecules left to measure.

00:03:50.227 --> 00:03:52.106
Its K a is too large to measure

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:54.666
in water, so tables simply list it as

00:03:54.747 --> 00:03:55.786
strong.

00:03:55.947 --> 00:03:59.066
So if the measured hydronium equals the concentration,

00:03:59.147 --> 00:04:00.906
the acid is strong.

00:04:00.947 --> 00:04:03.386
If it's well below, the acid is weak,

00:04:03.427 --> 00:04:05.146
and you can calculate its K a.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:08.766
K a also tells you about the conjugate

00:04:08.847 --> 00:04:09.326
base.

00:04:09.807 --> 00:04:12.926
Ethanoate, the ion left behind, can take a

00:04:13.007 --> 00:04:15.806
proton back from water, and that reaction has

00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:16.926
its own K b.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:20.046
Now multiply K a by K b.

00:04:20.527 --> 00:04:23.726
The ethanoic acid and the ethanoate cancel, leaving

00:04:23.847 --> 00:04:27.486
hydronium times hydroxide, which is K w, one

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.726
times ten to the minus fourteen at twenty

00:04:29.807 --> 00:04:30.926
five degrees.

00:04:31.247 --> 00:04:34.126
So K b for ethanoate is K w

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:37.006
over K a: five point six times ten

00:04:37.087 --> 00:04:38.206
to the minus ten.

00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:42.713
Because K a times K b is fixed,

00:04:42.794 --> 00:04:45.593
a stronger acid has a weaker conjugate base.

00:04:46.234 --> 00:04:48.233
X minus has a K b of about

00:04:48.314 --> 00:04:50.153
one point eight times ten to the minus

00:04:50.314 --> 00:04:53.913
ten, smaller than ethanoate's, because H X is

00:04:53.994 --> 00:04:55.033
the stronger acid.

00:04:55.754 --> 00:04:57.993
This only works for a conjugate pair.

00:04:58.474 --> 00:05:01.833
Multiplying ethanoic acid's K a by ammonia's K

00:05:01.914 --> 00:05:03.833
b doesn't give K w.

00:05:05.034 --> 00:05:06.473
So here's what to remember.

00:05:06.954 --> 00:05:09.593
p K a is like golf: the lowest

00:05:09.674 --> 00:05:10.633
score wins.

00:05:11.114 --> 00:05:14.073
Lower p K a, bigger K a, stronger

00:05:14.154 --> 00:05:14.633
acid.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.593
And for a conjugate pair, K a times

00:05:17.674 --> 00:05:20.633
K b equals K w, so the stronger

00:05:20.674 --> 00:05:23.273
the acid, the weaker its conjugate base.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.460
Your turn.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.420
Lactic acid is the weak acid that forms

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.260
when milk turns sour.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.220
A zero point two zero zero moles per

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.100
litre solution of lactic acid has a pH

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.180
of two point two eight at twenty five

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:39.460
degrees.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.620
Calculate the K a and the p K

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.260
a of lactic acid.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:44.820
Pause here to answer.

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:57.713
Hydronium is ten to the minus two point

00:05:57.794 --> 00:06:00.673
two eight, five point two five times ten

00:06:00.754 --> 00:06:02.033
to the minus three.

00:06:02.354 --> 00:06:04.673
The acid left is about zero point one

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:05.953
nine five.

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:08.993
K a is the hydronium squared over that:

00:06:09.074 --> 00:06:10.913
one point four times ten to the minus

00:06:11.074 --> 00:06:13.313
four, and the p K a is three

00:06:13.474 --> 00:06:14.913
point eight five.

00:06:14.994 --> 00:06:17.313
Stopping at the hydronium and calling it K

00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:20.033
a is the slip: K a isn't the

00:06:20.114 --> 00:06:20.993
hydronium.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:22.840
Last one.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.040
The p K a values of three acids

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.960
at twenty five degrees are: hydrofluoric acid, three

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.400
point one seven; methanoic acid, three point seven

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.960
four; and hydrocyanic acid, nine point two one.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.160
List the acids in order of decreasing strength.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.680
Then identify the strongest of their conjugate bases,

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.920
and calculate its K b.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.280
Pause here to answer.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:02.540
Lowest p K a wins: hydrofluoric acid, then

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.860
methanoic acid, then hydrocyanic acid.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:09.180
The weakest acid has the strongest conjugate base,

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.540
so that's cyanide.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.500
Hydrocyanic acid's K a is ten to the

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.300
minus nine point two one, six point two

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.020
times ten to the minus ten, so K

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.820
b is K w over that, one point

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.900
six times ten to the minus five.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.940
Putting nine point two one first gets golf

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:27.820
backwards.

00:07:29.034 --> 00:07:32.313
So to compare acid strength, compare K a

00:07:32.554 --> 00:07:35.833
or p K a, never pH alone unless

00:07:35.874 --> 00:07:37.273
the concentrations match.

00:07:37.914 --> 00:07:39.913
Get K a from a pH by running

00:07:39.994 --> 00:07:42.953
the ICE table backwards, convert with p K

00:07:43.034 --> 00:07:45.833
a equals negative log K a, and remember

00:07:45.914 --> 00:07:48.153
that lower p K a means stronger.

00:07:48.794 --> 00:07:51.193
For a conjugate pair, K b is K

00:07:51.274 --> 00:07:52.633
w over K a.

00:07:53.514 --> 00:07:56.553
Next: practice choosing the right method for weak

00:07:56.714 --> 00:07:57.673
acid questions.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.340
Here are the key notes.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.500
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:03.380
to copy them.
